<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- Curriculum sub-strand: Core 2.3 -->

<chapter xml:id="ch-loci" component="core" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Loci</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      A goat tied to a post by a rope can graze anywhere inside a circle. A path or region traced by points that obey a given rule is called a locus (plural: loci). You have already made the constructions that loci need, such as bisecting lines and angles.
    </p>
    <p>
      In this chapter you will construct the common loci: the perpendicular bisector, points at a given distance from a fixed point or from a fixed line, the angle bisector and the constant angle locus. You will then find where loci intersect, shade regions described by inequalities, and work with loci involving chords of a circle.
    </p>
    <p>
      Loci are used to decide where a borehole, a phone mast or a road can go so that it meets several conditions at once.
    </p>
  </introduction>

